    "engine": "knitr",
    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"What a crazy year\"\ndate: 2025-12-28\n# image: /assets/images/Aunicornis_habitus_CyanHot_AVG_241124.jpeg\ndescription: \"Wrapping up the craziest year I ever had.\"\ncategories:\n  - Journal\nfilters:\n  - openlinksinnewpage\n  - nutshell\nexecute:\n  echo: false\ncitation: false\nfeed:\n  categories:\n    - Journal\ndraft: true\ntoc: false\n# format:\n#   html:\n    # grid:\n    #   body-width: 1000px\n---\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n:::\n\n\n\n\n<!-- The aftershock is so strong, I still can’t believe everything that happened to me this year. The most precious thing I gained this year is not the one month on the Southern Ocean, not the summer week in Paris, not the crazy adventure from Iceland to Germany, even though they were all unbelievably wonderful and unforgettable, but an opportunity for entering the supportive, inclusive, and fantastic global communities of deep sea research, building connections with people from different backgrounds, expertise, associations, and affiliations, and becoming part of the peracarida and isopod taxonomists family.\n\nThis year started with two intensive weeks focusing on species description with our Argentine college, now my supervisor, Dr. Emanuel Pereira, followed by an adventure to the South Sandwich Islands with Ocean Census and Schmidt Ocean Institute. Coming back with fresh experience and excitement, I put my hands on drafting a manuscript with the coolest PhD student ever, now of course a real & real Dr. Jenny Neuhaus. Then I started my crazy side projects from Paris, where I met the Crustacean Society and had my first talk ever on an international conference (it could be better though). Followed by re-visiting Patagonia in August for the Ocean Census species discovery workshop in Punta Arenas, I spent three productive and fantastic weeks with enthusiastic and wonderful souls. Shortly after returning to Germany, our benthos team from DZMB HH made our way to Iceland to take over the 30 year-old legacy from BIOFAR and BIOICE. North Sea had concentrated all highlights for us in just five days, sailing through the Faroer Islands surrounded by mysterious fog, and the Shetland Islands with beautiful sunshine and birds and sheep in distance. We encountered endemic dolphins, we saw dancing northern light in the sky and swinging microbial illumination in the water. Landed on Wilhelmshaven, after I sent out sequencing that meant a lot for my thesis with my tight plan that's nothing else than sticking a needle in whenever there's room (it's a Chinese idiom: 見縫插針), a two-week Peracarida Workshop began, where I become the local organizing/helper.\n\nAfter all this, the crazy year which is described as \"turbulent\" by my supervisor, is finally becoming to it's final, more quiet phase. I gathered myself up, everything I saw, I heard, I experienced, I learned, and I thought, while I do experiments and looking at the tiny little isopods, I also spend a lot of time writing and archiving this year. -->\n\nLately on the phone with my mom, we were talking about the one-month photo shoot I did for my friend's second child. I suddenly can't remember when it was - was it this year? Was it in winter or in summer? These things happen a lot when I am stuck deeply in my temporal bubble. I have been busy non-stop since October, for the workshop, the thesis, the manuscript, the apartment, also for the future anxiety. \n\nLooking back, this year felt like eternity. So many things happened that I didn't make it to sort my mind and write things down. They are all connected with each other, but my memories pop up in pieces through the daily life, during the walk, the shower, or the daydream. In neuro science, I heard it's how the brain process memories, so maybe it's exactly the proof that they are connected - a web being knitted in my head.\n\nIt's not easy to share the exact thunderstorm of memory I have after sitting still for twenty minutes just thinking the past year of mine. But I will try show you the connection I made with the world this year.\n\n## The Cruise\n\nHave I ever dreamed to go to Antarctic? Of course, in my daydream. \n\nThe far far white land at the end of the world. It was too far away to actually dream about going there. It was one month after starting my internship at the DZMB. I was waiting if there's another berth for a cruise in New Zealand. Of course it was too late, but just about hours after they said no, my supervisor wrote me if I want to go to South Sandwich Islands. \n\nHey google, where is south sandwich island?\n\nIt turns out to be the middle of no where in the South Atlantic. Great. I'm going. \n\n\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<div id=\"roulette-fkt-animal-sfc\" class=\"roulette-container\" data-images=\"[{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://ik.imagekit.io/zzzhehao/assets/post/fkt250220-animal-sfc/fkt250220-animal-sfc-1.jpg&quot;},{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://ik.imagekit.io/zzzhehao/assets/post/fkt250220-animal-sfc/fkt250220-animal-sfc-2.jpg&quot;},{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://ik.imagekit.io/zzzhehao/assets/post/fkt250220-animal-sfc/fkt250220-animal-sfc-3.jpg&quot;},{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://ik.imagekit.io/zzzhehao/assets/post/fkt250220-animal-sfc/fkt250220-animal-sfc-4.jpg&quot;},{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://ik.imagekit.io/zzzhehao/assets/post/fkt250220-animal-sfc/fkt250220-animal-sfc-5.jpg&quot;},{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://ik.imagekit.io/zzzhehao/assets/post/fkt250220-animal-sfc/fkt250220-animal-sfc-6.jpg&quot;},{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://ik.imagekit.io/zzzhehao/assets/post/fkt250220-animal-sfc/fkt250220-animal-sfc-7.jpg&quot;},{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://ik.imagekit.io/zzzhehao/assets/post/fkt250220-animal-sfc/fkt250220-animal-sfc-8.jpg&quot;},{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://ik.imagekit.io/zzzhehao/assets/post/fkt250220-animal-sfc/fkt250220-animal-sfc-9.jpg&quot;},{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://ik.imagekit.io/zzzhehao/assets/post/fkt250220-animal-sfc/fkt250220-anima